{
  "text": "For any measurable spaces A and B with arbitrary σ-algebras, any probability measures μ and ν on A, any Markov kernel K from A to B, and any real number δ, suppose that |μ.real(S)−ν.real(S)| ≤ δ for every measurable set S ⊆ A. Then |(K∘ₘμ).real(T)−(K∘ₘν).real(T)| ≤ δ for every measurable set T ⊆ B. Here a Markov kernel assigns to every x ∈ A a probability measure K(x,·) on B, with x ↦ K(x,T) measurable for each measurable T. The measure K∘ₘρ on B is obtained by integrating the transition measures K(x,·) against ρ. The notation ρ.real(S) is the real value of the extended nonnegative event measure; the event measures in the displayed inequalities are finite under the stated probability and Markov assumptions. Absolute value and the inequalities are ordinary real operations. The same δ bounds every input event and every output event, without an additional factor. No separate sign condition on δ, topology, density, coupling, or further hypothesis is imposed.",
